fix(esmfold2-design): keep the preflight refusal intact through the seed fan-out, and stop the email blaming the customer's input

Both defects were found by review-code on the ESM-C checkpoint-pin delta
(5233d81b), not by a user report. The pin itself is unchanged.

1. The bucket did not survive a multi-seed run.

``run_tool`` is a pass-through for ``n_seeds == 1``, which is what all
three prod failures used, so the new ``preflight:weights`` refusal reached
the hub correctly there. For ``n_seeds >= 2`` it fans out, and
``_aggregate`` replaced every child's error with the bare string "All
seeds returned zero designs" whenever no designs completed. That string
matches no ``_FAILURE_RULES`` row, so a free, correctly-diagnosed refusal
was reported as "The run stopped for a reason we could not identify. Try
again with the same settings.", and ``classify_terminal_state`` wrote
``unclassified`` instead of ``preflight_miss``. ``n_seeds`` is a form
field (tools/esmfold2_design/__init__.py), so this was reachable by any
user, not only a campaign.

``_aggregate`` now forwards a child's own error, preferring a bucketed
dict over a bare string. The generic string remains for the case it was
written for: children that ran and produced nothing. Money was never at
risk -- a child that refuses at preflight writes no ``runtime_seconds``,
which ``modal_app.py:900`` reads as 0, and both failure classes are in
``_REFUNDED_FAILURE_CLASSES``.

2. The failure email contradicted the job page.

shared/email.py renders the failure email from ``failure_notice``, the
failure-CLASS sentence, while the job page renders ``failure_advice``,
the per-CHECK rule. The class sentence read "A check on the GPU machine
rejected the input before the run began." That is false for most of the
checks that route here -- esmfold2_design's ``preflight:weights`` is a
checkpoint broken on OUR Volume, af2 and colabfold emit
``preflight:jax-gpu``, mpnn emits ``preflight:module`` -- and for the
ESM-C mismatch it meant mailing our own broken Volume out as the
customer's fault.

The sentence now says what happened and attributes nothing, which is the
only thing it can honestly do: mpnn also emits
``preflight:fixed_positions`` for "chain 'B' is not in the input PDB"
(tools/mpnn/run_pipeline.py:301-305), which genuinely IS the customer's
input, so the one shared sentence cannot be flipped to blame us either.
Attribution belongs to ``failure_advice``, which keys on the check.

Two earlier drafts of this message were wrong about WHY, and both were
caught by review-claims rather than by me.

The first claimed every check routing to ``preflight_miss`` is on the
``our_side`` row. FALSE: the row covers exactly env, weights, tmp, torch,
cuda, binary, transformers, payload, config, upload_urls_endpoint
(shared/jobs.py:1005-1011) and nothing else.

The second claimed the rest "match no row at all". Also FALSE, because
``_FAILURE_RULES`` matches the DETAIL text, not the check name. Measured
by routing each tool's real detail string through ``failure_advice``:

  preflight:weights              -> our_side
  preflight:jax-gpu "timed out"  -> timeout
  preflight:jax-gpu "(exit N)"   -> generic
  preflight:module               -> generic
  preflight:fixed_positions      -> generic

NOTE: ``_FAILURE_CLASS_PLAIN_WORDS`` is shared, so this changes the
preflight email for every tool that can emit a preflight bucket, not just
this one. For the three generic rows it also changes the page cause,
because generic renders this same sentence.

NOT fixed here, and pre-existing: those three generic rows carry
``retry_unchanged=True``, and the timeout row is worse than generic. A
user whose chain name is wrong is told "Try again with the same
settings", and a jax-gpu timeout -- ours, not theirs -- is told to "ask
for fewer designs, or crop the target". Both are the same shape as
telling someone to change their seed when the seed cannot matter. In
mpnn, af2 and colabfold rather than here.

Tests. Five added, all mutation-proven:
  * the multi-seed refusal keeps its bucket (fails pre-fix with
    error_bucket=None);
  * the generic string still covers barren-but-successful children;
  * end-to-end over the real seams -- _aggregate ->
    _interpret_pipeline_return -> classify_terminal_state ->
    failure_advice -- asserting "not because of your input" and that the
    word "seed" is absent from the fix text;
  * the shared preflight sentence attributes fault to neither side, with
    both halves of the fault line asserted against the ``our_side`` row;
  * ``?pilot=1`` still serves 200 on every tool whose PILOT is None. Both
    existing pilot tests ``continue`` past such a tool, so no test had
    ever loaded that URL -- and esmfold2-design's withdrawn pilot leaves
    every old bookmark pointing at it. Not keyed on esmfold2-design: that
    withdrawal is written down as reversible.

